ormative stuff and just say well  we're going to teach the facts and then  we're going to help people to make their  own decisions and yet I think given the  scale again at the problem I think we  should reconsider this and again I think  we can look back at the civil rights  movement so think about us as a  land-grant institution and think about  the 50s or 60s and you know we would  think well our role is to do and it's  too bad Scott Peters isn't here because  he would have a lot to say about this  but our role is justice is to sort of  you know transfer what's known about  race or something like that based on the  science and if you look back on it now  we might say that would have been quite  irresponsible or even immoral so is  there a parallel here with climate  change  and then the last its climate changes in  the new normal I talked about that  earlier explicitly acknowledged and we  can't really conserve our natural  heritage at least in many cases as we  might have tried to do before so I said  I'd present three different projects but  I'm going to do a little bit more in  depth on this last project in particular  I want to talk about adaptation in  relation to climate changes the new  normal so climate change simply means  adjustments in ecological social or  excuse me climate adaptation means  adjustments in ecological social or  economic systems in response to climate  change and if you look at the IPCC  report that just came out this year they  have three different major categories of  adaptation options so there's physical  structural social which has to do with  equity issues and then institutional  which has to do with changing government  agencies or nonprofits or international  organizations but within each of those  categories they have sub categories that  I think and I've just chosen the ones  that I think are most consistent with  environmental education so  ecosystem-based under physical  structural community-based and education  under social and engagement under  institutional by the way this I just  picked up last night I think it was on a  BBC article and it's about but it's  Boston re-envisioning itself as Venice  so this is actually yeah they mentioned  Amsterdam also  okay so ecosystem-based adaptation is  the use of biodiversity and ecosystem  services to help communities than people  adapt to climate change and so we can  think of again this falls under physical  structural so we can think of sea walls  or putting all our you know utilities on  the second floor of buildings where  they're on flood zones or high water  zones but we can also think about  plantings oyster reefs oyster  restoration is a huge effort on our  restoration in New York City estuary  right now is sort of more consistent  with the environmental values of  environmental education and community  based adaptation turn locally driven  adaptation strategies that operate on  learning by doing bottom-up and  empowerment paradigm and here I like to  think of community gardens as a really  good example of a community-based  adaptation and the reason is because I  think people in community gardens they  engage in these kinds of discussions as  Megan knows and this helped them with  and they have to make a lot of decisions  right so they have to decide okay how do  we decide who gets a plot how do we  decide on whether pesticides are allowed  and if somebody lets their weeds grow  what kind of sanctions are we going to  have and there's been some work done by  Erika svensson in New York City at that  Forest Service Urban Research Station  looking at how what goes on this  decision making in these little bit  little kind of interventions and the  powerman that people get how it scales  up so these are I'd like to think of  these as kind of training grounds and a  lot of times the people that are in  community gardens are immigrants and  they may not have participated in civil  society previously and then you know  sometimes people that have been in these  gardens and Ben decision-making roles or  participated in these discussions then  go on and they become advocates at a  larger scale like for preserving green  space in New York City  so I think that there's a lot of these  kinds of civic ecology practices or  greening practices that we can think of  as combining ecosystem-based  community-based and education adaptation  in other words they provide  opportunities for Environmental Learning  even though there night not be a formal  education program there and these can be  things like restoring wetlands and salt  marshes to buffer against sea level rise  and installing green infrastructure such  as this green roof garden in new york  city as well as porous pavements and  community gardens so getting back to my  original question was super storm sandy  a tipping point for environmental  education probably not but i do think  that there is a lot of new ideas being  generated that we can follow up on and  so we are seeing changes in EE and some  of the major changes i think worth it  seeing are the use of the term  resilience and people starting to think  about adaptation these environmental  stewardship like the dune restoration  practices more thinking about collective  action and then reconsidering the notion  of advocacy given the scale of the  issues so with that I'd like to thank my  colleagues Bryce Du Bois Judy browse and  Ferguson and John Fraser who have helped  me think about some of these issues as  well as my funders but because I talked  about advocacy views expressed or not  necessarily those of the funding  agencies so if you're so much memory  thank you  this has been a production of Cornell  University on the web at cornell edu


